<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <article xml:id="mod1-video-outlines">
    <title>Module 1: Video Lecture Outlines</title>
    <subtitle>Three Lightboard Videos — Instructor Reference</subtitle>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This document contains full outlines for the three Module 1 video
        lectures. Each outline is written as an instructor script with
        lightboard cues, suggested examples to write out, and timing
        guidance. Target length for each video is 7&#x2013;10 minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Format note.</alert> Sections labeled
        <q><em>Write on board:</em></q> indicate what should appear on the
        lightboard during that segment. Sections labeled
        <q><em>Say:</em></q> are narration cues; these need not be read
        verbatim but capture the key ideas to convey. Transition markers
        show suggested cuts or pauses.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-video1">
      <title>Video 1: Quantifiers and Mathematical Language</title>
      <subtitle>Target Length: 8 minutes</subtitle>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          <alert>Learning goal.</alert> Students can correctly read, write,
          translate, and negate statements involving <m>\forall</m> and
          <m>\exists</m>, including multi-quantifier statements.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Sources.</alert> Hirst <section-mark/>2.1&#x2013;2.2,
          <section-mark/>2.4; Zorn <section-mark/>1.4 (Proof Language lexicon
          and quantifiers subsection).
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v1-hook">
        <title>Hook (0:00&#x2013;0:45)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Here is a sentence that looks like mathematics but
          is actually meaningless: <q>For all <m>x</m>, <m>x^2 \geq 0</m>.</q>
          Wait<mdash/>that seems true. But true <em>for all</em> what?
          Real numbers? Complex numbers? Functions? The word <q>all</q>
          requires a universe. This is the first lesson in mathematical
          language: every quantified statement must specify what objects
          are being quantified.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <me>\forall x \in \mathbb{R}, \quad x^2 \geq 0</me>
          versus
          <me>\forall x \in \mathbb{C}, \quad x^2 \geq 0 \quad \text{(false!)}</me>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> The universe of discourse matters. In real analysis,
          our universe is almost always a subset of <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, but
          we must always say so explicitly.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v1-symbols">
        <title>The Two Quantifiers (0:45&#x2013;2:30)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\forall</m> means <q>for all</q> / <q>for every</q> /
          <q>for each</q> / <q>given any</q>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\exists</m> means <q>there exists</q> / <q>there is</q> /
          <q>we can find</q> / <q>for some</q>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> These symbols are shorthand for claims of universality
          and existence. The universal quantifier <m>\forall</m> makes a
          claim about <em>every</em> element of a set. The existential
          quantifier <m>\exists</m> claims at least one element has a property.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board (two examples side by side):</em>
          <md>
            <mrow>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \exists N \in \mathbb{N}
            \text{ s.t. } n \geq N \Rightarrow |a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon
            \quad &amp;\text{(definition of } a_n \to L\text{)}</mrow>
            <mrow>\exists x \in \mathbb{R} \text{ s.t. } x^2 = 2
            \quad &amp;\text{(existence of } \sqrt{2}\text{)}</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Notice that the first example is how we define what it
          means for a sequence to converge. You will see this definition
          repeatedly this summer. Every symbol in it is essential. Changing
          even one changes the meaning entirely.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v1-order">
        <title>Order of Quantifiers: It Matters (2:30&#x2013;4:15)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <md>
            <mrow>&amp;(A) \quad \forall x \in \mathbb{R} \;\;
            \exists n \in \mathbb{N} \text{ s.t. } x \lt n</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;(B) \quad \exists n \in \mathbb{N} \text{ s.t. }
            \forall x \in \mathbb{R},\;\; x \lt n</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Statement (A) says: <q>Every real number is smaller
          than <em>some</em> natural number.</q> This is true<mdash/>it is
          equivalent to the Archimedean property. Statement (B) says:
          <q>There is a single natural number larger than every real number.</q>
          This is false<mdash/>obviously, since the reals are unbounded above.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Key message (write on board):</em>
          <me>\forall x \; \exists y \neq \exists y \; \forall x</me>
          Swapping the order of <em>different</em> quantifiers changes the meaning.
          Swapping the order of <em>same</em> quantifiers does not.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> This is not a pedantic point. The difference between
          <q>pointwise continuity</q> and <q>uniform continuity</q> in
          MAT 5610 is exactly a swap of quantifiers. We will see this in
          Module 6.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v1-negation">
        <title>Negation of Quantified Statements (4:15&#x2013;6:15)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Negating a quantified statement is one of the most
          important skills in analysis. You will need it every time you want
          to prove a limit does <em>not</em> equal something, or a sequence
          does <em>not</em> converge. Here are the rules:
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <md>
            <mrow>\neg(\forall x, P(x)) \;&amp;\equiv\;
            \exists x \text{ s.t. } \neg P(x)</mrow>
            <mrow>\neg(\exists x, P(x)) \;&amp;\equiv\;
            \forall x, \neg P(x)</mrow>
          </md>
          <q>Not all</q> = <q>there exists one that isn't.</q>
          <br/>
          <q>There does not exist</q> = <q>none of them have it.</q>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Example (write on board):</em> Negate the convergence definition:
          <md>
            <mrow>\text{Original: } &amp;\forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;
            \exists N \in \mathbb{N} \text{ s.t. } n \geq N
            \Rightarrow |a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon</mrow>
            <mrow>\text{Negation: } &amp;\exists \varepsilon \gt 0
            \text{ s.t. } \forall N \in \mathbb{N},
            \exists n \geq N \text{ s.t. } |a_n - L| \geq \varepsilon</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> This negation is the definition of
          <m>a_n \not\to L</m>. Notice: every <m>\forall</m> became
          <m>\exists</m>, every <m>\exists</m> became <m>\forall</m>, and
          the final inequality flipped.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v1-prose">
        <title>Mathematical Prose: Two Writing Rules (6:15&#x2013;7:45)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Two rules from Zorn worth remembering before we
          move on.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em> Rule 1: <alert>Use standard notation consistently.</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>(1, 3)</m>, <m>[1, 3]</m>, <m>\{1, 3\}</m> have three
          different meanings. Do not mix them up.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em> Rule 2: <alert>Watch word order.</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
          <q>For every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there exists <m>n</m> such
          that <m>1/n \lt \varepsilon</m></q>
          is true. <q>There exists <m>n</m> such that for every
          <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, <m>1/n \lt \varepsilon</m></q>
          is false.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Mathematical prose is not poetry. It is closer to
          formal logic written in English. Every word carries weight.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v1-close">
        <title>Closing (7:45&#x2013;8:00)</title>
        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> We have covered quantifiers, their order, negation,
          and two rules for clear mathematical prose. In Video 2 we will
          build on this to talk about proof strategies: how you take a
          mathematical claim and decide how to attack it.
        </p>
      </subsection>

    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-video2">
      <title>Video 2: Proof Strategies&#x2014;Direct, Contrapositive, Contradiction</title>
      <subtitle>Target Length: 9 minutes</subtitle>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          <alert>Learning goal.</alert> Students can identify when to use
          direct proof, proof by contrapositive, or proof by contradiction,
          and can execute each strategy correctly.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Sources.</alert> Hirst <section-mark/>3.5&#x2013;3.6;
          Zorn <section-mark/>1.5 (Direct Proof, Indirect Proof, Proof by
          Contradiction sections).
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v2-setup">
        <title>The Shape of a Claim (0:00&#x2013;1:00)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Most mathematical claims in real analysis have the
          shape <q>If <m>P</m>, then <m>Q</m></q> or can be rewritten in
          that shape. Before choosing a proof strategy, you need to identify
          two things: what are you <em>assuming</em>, and what are you
          <em>trying to show</em>?
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <md>
            <mrow>&amp;\text{Claim: If } P, \text{ then } Q.</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\text{Assume: } P \quad\quad \text{Show: } Q</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Once you have identified <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m>,
          you have three main options.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v2-direct">
        <title>Direct Proof (1:00&#x2013;3:00)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <me>\text{Direct: Assume } P. \text{ Deduce } Q.</me>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> A direct proof starts by assuming <m>P</m> and
          then uses definitions, previously known results, and algebra
          to derive <m>Q</m>. It is the default strategy. Try it first.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Example (write as proof on board):</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Claim.</alert> If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are rational,
          then <m>a + b</m> is rational.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Proof.</alert> Since <m>a</m> is rational, we can write
          <m>a = p/q</m> for integers <m>p, q</m> with <m>q \neq 0</m>.
          Since <m>b</m> is rational, write <m>b = r/s</m> for integers
          <m>r, s</m> with <m>s \neq 0</m>. Then
          <me>a + b = \frac{p}{q} + \frac{r}{s} = \frac{ps + rq}{qs}.</me>
          Since integers are closed under addition and multiplication, the
          numerator <m>ps + rq</m> and denominator <m>qs</m> are integers,
          and <m>qs \neq 0</m>. Thus <m>a + b</m> is rational.
          <m>\square</m>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Notice the structure: (1) unpack the hypothesis
          using the definition, (2) compute, (3) verify the conclusion
          by appealing to the definition again.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v2-contra">
        <title>Proof by Contrapositive (3:00&#x2013;5:30)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <md>
            <mrow>&amp;\text{Contrapositive of ``If } P \text{ then } Q\text{'':}</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\text{``If not } Q \text{ then not } P.\text{''}</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\text{Strategy: Assume } \neg Q. \text{ Deduce } \neg P.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> A claim and its contrapositive are logically
          equivalent<mdash/>they have the same truth value. So proving
          the contrapositive proves the claim. Use this strategy when
          <m>\neg Q</m> is more concrete and workable than <m>P</m>,
          or when <m>\neg P</m> is a natural conclusion.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Example (write as proof on board):</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Claim.</alert> If <m>n^2</m> is odd, then <m>n</m>
          is odd.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Try direct: we'd assume <m>n^2</m> is odd and
          try to conclude <m>n</m> is odd<mdash/>but this is awkward. The
          contrapositive says: if <m>n</m> is even, then <m>n^2</m> is
          even. That is much more concrete.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Proof (by contrapositive).</alert> Assume <m>n</m> is
          even. Then <m>n = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>, so
          <m>n^2 = (2k)^2 = 4k^2 = 2(2k^2)</m>, which is even.
          Thus: <m>n</m> even <m>\Rightarrow n^2</m> even, i.e.,
          <m>n^2</m> odd <m>\Rightarrow n</m> odd. <m>\square</m>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Notice that we announced our strategy
          (<q>by contrapositive</q>), assumed the negation of the
          conclusion, and derived the negation of the hypothesis.
          Always tell your reader what strategy you are using.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v2-contradiction">
        <title>Proof by Contradiction (5:30&#x2013;7:45)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <md>
            <mrow>&amp;\text{Contradiction: Assume } P \text{ and } \neg Q.</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\text{Derive a contradiction.}</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\text{Conclude: } Q \text{ must hold.}</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> In a proof by contradiction, you assume the
          <em>opposite</em> of what you want to prove and show that leads
          to an absurdity. Use this strategy when the existence of a
          counterexample would have very specific and restrictive
          consequences that you can then refute.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Example (write key steps on board):</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Claim.</alert> <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is irrational.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Proof.</alert> Assume, toward contradiction, that
          <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is rational. Write <m>\sqrt{2} = p/q</m>
          in lowest terms (<m>p, q \in \mathbb{Z}</m>, <m>q \neq 0</m>,
          <m>\gcd(p,q) = 1</m>). Then
          <me>2 = \frac{p^2}{q^2} \implies p^2 = 2q^2.</me>
          So <m>p^2</m> is even, hence <m>p</m> is even (by the previous
          example). Write <m>p = 2m</m>. Then
          <m>4m^2 = 2q^2</m>, so <m>q^2 = 2m^2</m>, so <m>q</m> is
          also even. But then <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> share the factor 2,
          contradicting <m>\gcd(p,q) = 1</m>. <m>\square</m>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Proof by contradiction is especially useful for
          claims about irrationality, non-existence, or uniqueness.
          Notice the phrase <q>assume, toward contradiction</q><mdash/>this
          signals to your reader exactly what strategy you are using and
          what to look for.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v2-close">
        <title>Summary and Heuristics (7:45&#x2013;9:00)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
        </p>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Strategy</cell>
            <cell>Use when&#x2026;</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Direct</cell>
            <cell><m>P</m> gives you something to work with directly</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Contrapositive</cell>
            <cell><m>\neg Q</m> is more concrete than <m>P</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Contradiction</cell>
            <cell>Assuming <m>\neg Q</m> has very specific consequences</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> In Video 3, we take up a fourth strategy:
          mathematical induction<mdash/>the workhorse for proving
          statements about all natural numbers.
        </p>
      </subsection>

    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-video3">
      <title>Video 3: Mathematical Induction</title>
      <subtitle>Target Length: 8 minutes</subtitle>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          <alert>Learning goal.</alert> Students can write a complete
          induction proof, correctly identify the base case and inductive
          step, and recognize common induction errors.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Sources.</alert> Hirst <section-mark/>3.3&#x2013;3.4;
          Zorn <section-mark/>1.5 (Proof by Induction section).
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v3-idea">
        <title>The Idea Behind Induction (0:00&#x2013;1:30)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Mathematical induction is how we prove statements
          of the form <q><m>P(n)</m> holds for every natural number
          <m>n</m>.</q> Think of a row of dominoes: if the first one
          falls, and each falling domino knocks over the next one, then
          all the dominoes eventually fall. Induction works the same way.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <md>
            <mrow>&amp;\textbf{Step 1 (Base Case): } P(1) \text{ is true.}</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\textbf{Step 2 (Inductive Step): }
            \text{Assume } P(n) \text{ is true.}</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad
            \text{Prove } P(n+1) \text{ is true.}</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\textbf{Conclusion: } P(n) \text{ holds for all }
            n \in \mathbb{N}.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> The assumed statement <m>P(n)</m> in the inductive
          step is called the <term>induction hypothesis</term>. You may
          use it freely in proving <m>P(n+1)</m>.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v3-example">
        <title>A Classic Example (1:30&#x2013;4:30)</title>

        <p>
          <alert>Claim.</alert> For all <m>n \geq 1</m>,
          <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement
          <q><m>\sum_{k=1}^n k = n(n+1)/2</m>.</q>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board (base case):</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Base case</alert> <m>(n = 1)</m>: The left side is
          <m>\sum_{k=1}^1 k = 1</m>. The right side is
          <m>1 \cdot 2 / 2 = 1</m>. These are equal, so <m>P(1)</m>
          holds. <m>\checkmark</m>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board (inductive step):</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Inductive step.</alert> Assume <m>P(n)</m>: suppose
          <m>\sum_{k=1}^n k = n(n+1)/2</m>. We must show <m>P(n+1)</m>:
          <m>\sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k = (n+1)(n+2)/2</m>. Compute:
          <md>
            <mrow>\sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k
            \;&amp;=\; \left(\sum_{k=1}^n k\right) + (n+1)</mrow>
            <mrow>\;&amp;=\; \frac{n(n+1)}{2} + (n+1)
            \quad\text{(induction hypothesis)}</mrow>
            <mrow>\;&amp;=\; (n+1)\left(\frac{n}{2} + 1\right)
            = (n+1) \cdot \frac{n+2}{2}
            = \frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}.</mrow>
          </md>
          This is exactly <m>P(n+1)</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em> Conclusion: by induction, <m>P(n)</m>
          holds for all <m>n \geq 1</m>. <m>\square</m>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Notice the three clearly labeled parts: base case,
          inductive step (with the hypothesis explicitly stated), and
          conclusion. Always include all three.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v3-pitfalls">
        <title>Induction Pitfalls (4:30&#x2013;6:30)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> Induction proofs fail in two common ways.
          Hirst calls these <q>inductive pitfalls.</q>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <alert>Pitfall 1: No base case.</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
          If you skip or get the base case wrong, the whole proof collapses.
          Hirst's example: <q>Non-Theorem: <m>3^n \geq n!</m>.</q>
          The inductive step works, but the statement fails for <m>n \geq 7</m>,
          and a correct base case check would reveal this.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <alert>Pitfall 2: Wrong base case.</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
          Classic example: <q>All horses are the same color.</q>
          The inductive step argument appears valid but fails at the
          transition from 1 to 2 horses<mdash/>the overlap required
          for the argument doesn't exist at the base case.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> The moral: the base case is not a formality.
          Check it carefully and make sure it genuinely holds before
          writing your conclusion.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v3-strong">
        <title>Strong Induction (Brief Mention) (6:30&#x2013;7:30)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> There is a variant called <term>strong induction</term>
          (or <term>complete induction</term>). In the inductive step you
          assume <m>P(k)</m> holds for <em>all</em> <m>k \leq n</m>, not
          just for <m>k = n</m>. This is useful when <m>P(n+1)</m> depends
          on more than just the previous case.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Write on board:</em>
          <md>
            <mrow>&amp;\textbf{Strong induction hypothesis:}</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\quad \text{Assume } P(1), P(2), \ldots, P(n)
            \text{ all hold.}</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;\quad \text{Prove } P(n+1).</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> For example, Zorn uses strong induction to show
          that every integer greater than 1 is either prime or has a prime
          factorization<mdash/>because factoring <m>n+1</m> may require
          knowing the structure of numbers much smaller than <m>n</m>.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
      <subsection xml:id="sub-v3-close">
        <title>Closing (7:30&#x2013;8:00)</title>

        <p>
          <em>Say:</em> That completes the three videos for Module 1.
          You now have the core tools: quantifiers and their negations,
          three proof strategies, and induction. The study guide, worked
          examples, and practice set will give you the chance to use
          and consolidate these tools before the module assessment.
        </p>
      </subsection>

    </section>

  </article>

</pretext>
